WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.295
A checkout request fails.

2
00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:07.423
Then another.

3
00:00:07.427 --> 00:00:11.168
Your dashboards show the error
rate climbing, but not why.

4
00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:14.877
So someone opens a trace,
scrolls through a hundred spans,

5
00:00:14.773 --> 00:00:18.150
and goes hunting through the logs
for anything that looks related.

6
00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:20.477
Tayga does that first pass for you.

7
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:24.372
Tayga runs beside your stack.

8
00:00:24.194 --> 00:00:28.978
Your OpenTelemetry Collector sends
it traces and logs over OTLP,

9
00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:31.026
with no proprietary agent.

10
00:00:30.812 --> 00:00:36.553
Tayga buffers them in Redpanda, closes each
trace ten seconds after its last span,

11
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.935
stores everything in ClickHouse, and analyses
every request that failed or ran slow.

12
00:00:41.722 --> 00:00:45.099
The core is open source, under the AGPL.

13
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.386
Let's break something.

14
00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:51.525
The OpenTelemetry demo runs next to Tayga.

15
00:00:51.325 --> 00:00:55.307
We turn on its paymentFailure
flag, so every charge fails,

16
00:00:55.106 --> 00:00:58.495
and watch the Stories page
refresh in live mode.

17
00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:00.023
In our end-to-end runs,

18
00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:04.859
the first payment story arrived
45 to 95 seconds after the flip.

19
00:01:04.682 --> 00:01:09.368
And here it is: payment charge
failed, a new error story group.

20
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.472
Open the story.

21
00:01:12.294 --> 00:01:17.174
At the top, the root cause: the service
and span where the failure started,

22
00:01:16.973 --> 00:01:18.472
with its message.

23
00:01:18.258 --> 00:01:23.708
Then the request path, through checkout
to payment, the failing service in red.

24
00:01:23.494 --> 00:01:27.962
The Compared with normal panel lists what
differs from a typical request to this

25
00:01:27.785 --> 00:01:28.944
endpoint.

26
00:01:28.731 --> 00:01:31.853
Here, the operations that never got to run.

27
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.302
The waterfall opens on the critical
path, with the root-cause span selected.

28
00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:40.399
And below, the logs this trace
wrote, each linked to its template.

29
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:46.235
Stories with the same fingerprint,
the same kind, endpoint,

30
00:01:46.058 --> 00:01:50.053
root-cause span and masked
message, form one group.

31
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.569
Numbers, IDs and hex strings are masked,
so repeated failures become one row,

32
00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:57.399
with a count and a trend.

33
00:01:57.185 --> 00:02:02.090
The inspector previews the latest story,
so you can triage with the arrow keys.

34
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:08.005
The service map draws every service
in call order, with its call rate,

35
00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:10.489
error ratio and p99.

36
00:02:10.288 --> 00:02:14.441
A service turns red when 5 %
or more of its calls fail,

37
00:02:14.264 --> 00:02:19.241
and amber when its p99 is more
than twice its 24-hour level.

38
00:02:19.136 --> 00:02:23.241
Select one for its charts, and the
stories whose root cause is there.

39
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:27.065
Tayga mines your logs too.

40
00:02:26.888 --> 00:02:30.738
Lines that differ only in their
variable parts become templates,

41
00:02:30.524 --> 00:02:34.556
at about 2.8 µs per line in our benchmark.

42
00:02:34.342 --> 00:02:38.968
It alerts on a new template, a
spike above baseline, and silence,

43
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:41.065
for templates you choose to watch.

44
00:02:40.851 --> 00:02:43.392
Each alert links to example traces,

45
00:02:43.178 --> 00:02:47.647
and the notifier sends it to Slack
or any webhook, with retries.

46
00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:51.538
And Tayga watches itself.

47
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.168
The Pipeline page shows whether each
service is up, charts every stage,

48
00:02:55.967 --> 00:03:01.138
and reads consumer lag straight from
Kafka, all collected by the API itself,

49
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.028
with no Prometheus needed.

50
00:03:02.851 --> 00:03:04.410
Prefer a light theme?

51
00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:05.574
One click.

52
00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:09.581
Getting started takes one line.

53
00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:13.944
The installer checks Docker, starts
Tayga with ClickHouse and Redpanda,

54
00:03:13.731 --> 00:03:16.635
and prints the Collector
snippet for your data.

55
00:03:16.422 --> 00:03:19.217
On Kubernetes, there's a Helm chart.

56
00:03:19.112 --> 00:03:24.017
Or run it next to the OpenTelemetry
demo and flip a flag, like we just did.

57
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.723
To try it yourself, start with
the quickstart on the docs site.

58
00:03:29.545 --> 00:03:34.595
Tayga: from a failing request to
its root cause, in one story.
